To understand how a financial application can develop a negative balance despite rejecting negative inputs, you need to study all the possible failure categories across its transaction lifecycle.

There is no finite universal list of every implementation-specific bug, but the following is a comprehensive security-research taxonomy.

### 1\. Transaction and business-logic vulnerabilities

| No. | Vulnerability                         | What can go wrong                                  |
| --- | ------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------- |
| 1   | Insufficient-funds validation failure | Withdrawal exceeds available balance               |
| 2   | Incorrect balance calculation         | System calculates available funds incorrectly      |
| 3   | Stale balance                         | Transaction uses an outdated balance               |
| 4   | Missing transaction limits            | Withdrawal exceeds permitted limits                |
| 5   | Incorrect transaction ordering        | Debit occurs before a required credit              |
| 6   | Incorrect reversal                    | Reversal creates an extra debit                    |
| 7   | Refund inconsistency                  | Refund is calculated or applied incorrectly        |
| 8   | Fee calculation error                 | Fees cause the balance to become negative          |
| 9   | Interest calculation error            | Incorrect interest adjustment changes the balance  |
| 10  | Overdraft-rule error                  | System applies an unintended overdraft             |
| 11  | Pending transaction omission          | Reserved funds are treated as available            |
| 12  | Authorization-state error             | Transaction executes without valid approval        |
| 13  | Transaction-state inconsistency       | Failed, pending and completed states are confused  |
| 14  | Duplicate transaction                 | One operation is accounted for more than once      |
| 15  | Partial transaction failure           | Some financial operations commit while others fail |

